set('PLN-06','PARTIAL','Wave 3 iterations 6-8 let the planner author and edit working-day duration plus multiple employee, vehicle, asset and governed shared-pool demand lines on a WBS-linked activity, then commit an exact persisted requirement into a held booking. The service derives resource, unit, quantity and dates from the saved activity, PostgreSQL protects requirement lineage, and the Auth-ON browser proves create, reload, cross-project conflict, reason-governed over-capacity commitment and retained release history. A held employee booking now also reaches the linked person’s My Work (PLN-07). HR/Fleet availability remains open.','Connect HR/Fleet availability so a leave or breakdown changes the feasibility of an existing commitment without re-entry.','handoff');
set('PLN-07','PARTIAL','A held commitment now reaches the person answerable for it, whichever of the three kinds of resource it names. PERSON: an administrator binds an employment record to a platform account in HR (migration 0317) — one account per record, unregistered/deactivated/already-claimed accounts refused, provenance recorded — and the employee’s allocation arrives in their My Work carrying project, activity, quantity and dates, where they accept or decline it (migration 0318). CREW: a pool carries a named roster (migration 0319, membership kept strictly apart from capacity) and a crew booking reaches every current member as news about their crew, carrying no answer because one member cannot speak for a crew. EQUIPMENT: the owning register names one person per machine — Fleet’s driver, and an asset’s custodian (migration 0320) — and that person answers for the MACHINE, not for their own time. Every answer is the same fifth fact: a refusal needs a reason, changes no quantity, status or capacity, and surfaces on the planning desk beside the capacity verdict. Handing equipment on, or leaving a crew, stops the commitment reaching that person while the commitment itself stands. One Auth-ON browser journey proves the whole chain, including a second claim on one account (409), an unregistered account (400), an HR-read role refused the link authority (403), a reasonless decline (400), one person answering for another (403), a member answering for a crew (403), answering for equipment no longer held (403), a duplicate roster entry (409), an invented employee id (400) and custody given to a stranger (400). An allocated non-member still gets no project access from being booked.','Give an allocated non-member a governed path to the project access the work implies.','handoff');
for (const layer of ['domain','persistence','api','permissions','ui','actualOutput','browser','handoff']) caps.find(c=>c.id==='PLN-07').layers[layer]='PARTIAL';
set('PLN-08','PARTIAL','The live schedule records vehicle/asset demand from canonical Fleet/Assets catalogs and commits it into typed, dated bookings. An asset now carries a custodian in its own register (migration 0320, shaped like Fleet’s existing driver link), so an equipment commitment reaches the one person answerable for that machine and is accepted or declined by them with a reason — proven Auth-ON in the browser, including the refusal for somebody who no longer holds it. Breakdown and availability are still not bridged: a machine going out of service does not change the feasibility of a commitment already made against it.','Bridge Fleet/Assets availability and breakdown into current feasibility, so a machine out of service surfaces as a conflict on the commitments that depend on it.','handoff');
set('PLN-09','PARTIAL','The second half of the temporal invariant is connected. HR, Fleet and Assets now state availability through a port §22 reads and does not own (ADR-0004, bound at the composition root): an APPROVED leave, a scheduled service visit and a retired vehicle or disposed asset are dated ABSENCES — a known zero — while an undated “out of service” status is UNKNOWN, because nobody said until when, and unknown is never available. A standing commitment therefore becomes CONFLICTED when the owning register later says the resource is not there, the verdict names the cause in the register’s own words, and `becameInfeasible` distinguishes “this fitted when it was made” from “this never fitted”. Nothing is vetoed: the leave is still approved, the booking is neither rejected nor altered, and not one field of the commitment changes. A pending leave is a request and is ignored; a pool never inherits its members’ leave, because deriving crew capacity from a roster is arithmetic nobody stated. An unbound or failing provider reads as silence, never as availability. Auth-ON browser proof drives HR from request to approval and watches the planning desk change. A named conflict-resolution owner, and links from a conflict to the other project’s activities, remain open.','Give each surfaced conflict a named resolution owner and an authorized link to every project activity involved in it.','handoff');
for (const layer of ['domain','persistence','api','permissions','ui','actualOutput','browser','handoff']) caps.find(c=>c.id==='PLN-09').layers[layer]='PARTIAL';
set('PLN-10','COMPLETE','Conflicts are detected across projects from §22’s own facts and from the owning registers, and over-allocation is governed rather than blocked — committing past a KNOWN capacity costs a written reason. A conflict carries PEOPLE: a named owner takes a resource’s conflicts on for a period (migration 0321, one open owner per resource) and closes them with what was decided, kept apart as `resolved` or `accepted`. It reaches the OTHER SIDE: each commitment carries the competing commitments resolved along the canonical chain — resource, booking, requirement, schedule activity, WBS node, project — every link read from a stored row and never from anything the caller supplies, with every overlapping party returned rather than the first. Authorization is applied server-side and separately from conflict ownership: `projects.resource-conflict.*` opens no project’s plan, so a party in a project the reader may not open arrives as a `restricted` commitment carrying its size and overlap and no project, activity or work package at all — and the cross-project report’s own contributors and project list are redacted by the same rule, with the involved count kept honest. The relationship is wholly derived: it disappears when the other commitment moves or is released and reappears naming a different activity, while the ownership and decision recorded about it survive untouched; nothing on the conflict path writes to a booking, schedule, project or availability.','Promoted on 2026-09-15 by the programme owner after reconciliation against every acceptance criterion this capability has carried (two-project employee and equipment bookings, leave and breakdown conflicts, authorized resolution, visible planner action, named resolver, recorded resolution, and the canonical authorized link to every activity involved) — the table is in the Wave 3 report. Regression keeps the lineage/redaction unit proof, the conflict-ownership service proof and the Auth-ON browser journey green. KNOWINGLY ACCEPTED AND STILL OPEN: a conflict raises no notification to its owner, and no conflict register is exported as a document — which is why `actualOutput` remains PARTIAL rather than COMPLETE on an otherwise complete row.','handoff');
