set('PLN-11','COMPLETE','A schedule activity now reads what its work package was SOLD and PRICED for, and nobody types either. The rate the line was priced at is frozen with the award: at handover the accepted line’s rate build-up is read once and its manpower sheet normalised to ONE unit — crew size, man-hours per unit, crew-hours per unit — so a later revision selling a different quantity cannot distort it. IT CARRIES NO MONEY: hourly rates, margin and every amount stay behind `tendering.internal-pricing.access`, because how long work takes is a physical fact about the work while what it costs is a commercial one, and freezing the second would put the cost sheet inside every project anyone can open. Delivery then reaches back into Tendering for nothing — the basis is copied by value like every other award fact. On the read, an activity resolves the sold quantity, the priced rate, the quantity installed through the Quantity Ledger and the part of its own planned window already used, and reports the pace the work is going at against the pace it was sold at, with the rate now needed to still finish inside the window. Everything is DERIVED per read and stored nowhere: a stored “behind” would be behind as of the last refresh. UNKNOWN is neither zero nor “on rate” — a package with no award line, an award captured before the basis was frozen, a supply-only or subcontracted line that priced no crew, an activity with no usable window and a window that has not opened each come back UNKNOWN carrying the reason, while every fact that IS known is still reported beside it. The plan screen shows what was sold against what is in, the pace against the priced pace, and a headline count of the activities losing ground; an activity with no award line behind it stays silent rather than printing “unknown” under every bar. THE COST HALF IS NOW CONNECTED TOO. A labour allocation carries the work package its hours were spent on (migration 0323), checked at the service boundary through the resolver Projects registers at boot — a package in another project is refused at the point of writing, not discovered later by a report that drops the row. Projects reads those hours through a port bound at the composition root (ADR-0004), never importing Site, and sets three quantities of hours apart: PRICED (per unit, frozen with the award), EARNED (priced × installed — what the work should have taken) and SPENT (what the people who were there wrote down). The factor is earned ÷ spent, and it is a SEPARATE verdict from the pace, deliberately: a crew can be behind the programme and perfectly efficient, or exactly on rate and ruinous, and one number cannot carry both. NULL ATTRIBUTION IS NORMAL AND PERMANENT — mobilisation, standing time, a crew moving between three risers — so the share of the project’s hours naming no package travels WITH every figure, because a package credited with 40 of 500 man-hours looks superb until somebody is told about the other 460. No hours attributed at all reads UNKNOWN, never as infinite productivity, and hours spent before the first measure read UNKNOWN rather than a productivity of zero.','Auth-ON browser and HTTP proof through a real governed award priced at 2 technicians × 100 hours for 200 m² (→ 16 m²/day): the frozen basis carries crew and hours and NO key matching rate/cost/price/amount/margin; the activity reads 200 m² and 16/day with nothing entered; 60 m² installed ten days into a twenty-day window reads BEHIND at 6/day against 160 expected, naming the 14/day now needed; catching up to 160 reads ON_RATE; a package with no award line reads UNKNOWN and the screen stays silent; a supply-only line reads UNKNOWN ‘no crew was priced’ while still reporting what was sold and installed; and no rate, quantity or verdict is written onto the activity.  THE LABOUR HALF, proven Auth-ON in the same run: 160 m² installed at 1 priced man-hour each reads UNKNOWN ‘no labour has been attributed’ while still naming the 160 hours earned; a package in another project is refused (400); 40 electricians × 8 hours reads WORSE_THAN_PRICED at 320 spent against 160 earned (factor 0.5) while the pace verdict stays ON_RATE; and 80 further hours naming no package stay out of the figure and are reported beside it as 20% of the project’s hours. Twelve domain rules cover the edges, including the trap this exists to avoid — an unattributed package is never infinitely productive. STILL OPEN: several activities sharing one work package each inherit its whole sold quantity, because no apportionment has been authored and none can be inferred; and rates are counted in calendar days rather than working days, so a window spanning a shutdown flatters the achieved pace.','handoff');
