set('BUY-05','WRONG_BEHAVIOR','Reused J3-03: partial receipt marks full order received.','Receive 1 of 100, retain 99 outstanding and correct management exposure.','domain');
set('BUY-05','COMPLETE',"AN ORDER IS A SET OF POSITIONS, NOT A PERCENTAGE. The recorded defect — receiving one item of an order for a hundred marked the whole order received — had a cause the containment could not reach: the order carried ONE scalar quantity and no items, so “99 still outstanding” had nothing to be outstanding ON. Wave 4 iteration 2 stopped the false completion by refusing to conclude anything from an unknown quantity; this produces the true answer instead. Migration 0336 gave the order its lines and 0338 gave the delivery note its own, each receipt line answering ONE order line — `po_line_id` is required, because a receipt against nothing settles nothing. Each line is settled or still owed, and the order reaches `received` only when EVERY line is settled; a single figure across the order would say “most of it” and leave nobody able to tell which material to chase. ACCEPTED IS NOT DELIVERED: a rejected quantity arrived, was inspected and went back — real, recorded against the supplier, and NOT progress, because the material is still owed. Accepted and rejected are separate columns and only the accepted reduces what is outstanding; a rejection costs a reason, because one nobody explained cannot be acted on. The status is derived and returns NOTHING in two different situations that must not conclude — an order with no lines has no positions to measure, and an order where nothing has been accepted has not changed. Reconciliation runs on the receipt LINE rather than the note’s creation, because a note is created before anybody has written what is on it. The module seam is unchanged: Inventory says what arrived, Procurement decides what it means for the order.","Auth-ON HTTP and browser proof against PostgreSQL, JWT on. THE RECORDED CASE: receive 1 of 100 and the order is `partially_received`, never `received`; the remaining 99 on a SECOND note settles it, so receipts are cumulative across notes rather than per note. A SET OF POSITIONS: every camera received while the cable is still wholly owed leaves the order partial, and only the cable’s arrival completes it. ACCEPTED IS NOT DELIVERED: a wholly rejected delivery leaves the order untouched at `issued`, and a part-rejected one counts only its accepted part. REFUSALS: a rejection with no reason, a receipt line recording nothing arriving, and a receipt line naming no order line are each refused; an order with NO lines is left exactly where it is, because its position cannot be measured. EXPOSURE: outstanding quantity and outstanding value per line, at the price ordered, on the API and in the event payload. BROWSER, one Auth-ON run end to end: a requisition approved so its estimate is carried; the order showing 12 nr in the material’s own unit with the carried price marked “estimate — not agreed with a supplier” and provenance “Bought direct”; a buyer adding 250 m at an agreed price with no provisional marker; the order issued; a storekeeper recording 1 nr against the camera line from the goods-receipt register; the order page then reading “1 of 12 nr received, 11 outstanding” with the cable wholly outstanding, AED 1,600.00 still committed and the status settling at partially received; then 50 m rejected with a reason, shown as “50 m rejected — not received” beside the accepted figure with the cable still owed and the exposure unchanged. DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED ON THE WAY, all introduced by this work: a constructor parameter inserted mid-list rebound every later one in the cross-module reactor; the same parameter then injected NOTHING because a union-typed parameter emits `Object` in design:paramtypes — the defect PLN-04 already paid for on a milestone receipt — caught by the Auth-ON e2e rather than by review, since in-memory construction passes it positionally; and a hydration mismatch from formatting dates with the runtime’s own locale on a server-rendered client component, surfaced by the browser console. PERSISTENCE: `aura_inventory_goods_receipt_lines` RLS ENABLED and FORCED with a policy, verified live on a database rebuilt from zero (269 tenant-scoped tables, all enabled, forced and policied). ACCEPTED LIMITS: `BUY-05` has no next-role receipt clause of its own — its acceptance proof asks for the position and the exposure, both proved; the handoff it participates in is `BUY-07` (material delivery to work package), which remains open. Issue and return netting is `BUY-06` and is untouched here.");
for (const layer of ['domain','persistence','api','permissions','ui','actualOutput','browser','handoff']) caps.find(c=>c.id==='BUY-05').layers[layer]='COMPLETE';
set('BUY-06','WRONG_BEHAVIOR','Reused J3-02: issue/return dedupe collision.','Issue 20, return 5, retry both; net issued remains 15.','domain');
set('BUY-06','COMPLETE',"MATERIAL GOES TO A JOB, AND ONLY WHAT WENT CAN COME BACK. The recorded defect was an issue/return dedupe collision, and it is genuinely gone — the ledger post is keyed on the persisted movement id, so replaying an issue and a return onto the spine leaves the net where it was. But the half that made the record's second symptom true — “fresh proofs returned no ISSUED position” — was not a ledger defect at all: THE SCREEN COULD NOT ISSUE TO A JOB. The stock movement form posted a direction, a quantity, a reason and a cost and nothing else, while the quantity-ledger reactor begins `if (!boqItemId || !projectId) return`, so every movement a storekeeper could actually make posted nothing. The capability was unreachable from the screen it belongs on. The stock item now carries its own issue and return controls — a project, a BOQ item restricted to those with a baseline (issuing against an unmeasured item posts a quantity to nothing), and a quantity in the item's own unit. RETURNABLE IS THE GOVERNED NET ISSUED POSITION, NOT THE GROSS HISTORICAL ISSUE: 20 issued and 5 returned leaves 15 that can come back, even though 20 went out in total. A position that could not be read stays UNKNOWN and refuses — never coerced to zero, never implicit permission — because the dependency on the quantity ledger is optional by construction while the evidence never is. What is currently out there is shown before anything is typed, since it is the balance the server measures a return against, and with nothing issued returning is not offered. The module seam holds: Inventory owns the warehouse, the BOQ item's quantity position belongs to Projects, and Inventory declares `ISSUED_POSITION` for the composition root to bind rather than importing it.","Auth-ON HTTP and browser proof against PostgreSQL, JWT on. THE FROZEN SENTENCE, all four clauses: issue 20, return 5, net 15; RETRY BOTH — the issue AND the return replayed onto the spine exactly as the relay would after a failure, with the net unmoved. NET NOT GROSS: a return of 16 against a net of 15 refused 409 naming how much is actually out, and 15 then accepted to a net of 0. FAIL-CLOSED: a return against a project the material was never issued to is refused, and an unreadable position is refused rather than passed. An uncoded warehouse receipt is untouched by the rule — it is not a return from a project and has no issued balance to measure against. BROWSER, Auth-ON: nothing issued so returning is not offered; issue 20 and the panel reads “20 m currently issued … the most that can come back”; return 5 and it reads 15; a return of 16 refused in the domain's own words with the position unchanged; then the whole 15 comes back. A DEFECT MORE CONSEQUENTIAL THAN THE FROZEN SENTENCE, found by probing the running system and NOT asked for by the acceptance proof: a return was unbounded, so issue 20 / return 50 left the BOQ item reading issued −30, onSite 30 and wastage −30 with nothing refusing it, and progress, wastage and remaining-to-order all read from that position. It is fixed, and the fix is now pinned as a standing invariant rather than a slice test — `apps/api/src/issued-position-invariant.fitness.test.ts` locks net-not-gross, null-stays-UNKNOWN, the null guard preceding any arithmetic, an absent reader yielding null rather than 0, no `?? 0` coercion anywhere on the path, and the port seam; the gate was verified to FAIL when the fallback is changed back to 0. DEFECTS FOUND ON THE WAY: the panel read the eventually-consistent position once and showed a storekeeper a balance their own movement had already changed — it now polls briefly and stops either way; and `onSite` is `received − issued`, so my first assertion treated it as “what is on site” and was wrong — the test was corrected, not the code. ACCEPTED LIMITS, stated rather than absorbed: PERMISSIONS are proved only to the extent that an unauthenticated caller is refused 401 — there is no role-denial case, so the layer is PARTIAL, not COMPLETE. The BROWSER proof covers issue → return → net position. The full stock-accounting journey (receipt → available → issue → return → on-hand and valuation) is NOT a clause of this acceptance proof and is proved separately in `apps/web/e2e/stock-accounting-journey.spec.ts`; `BUY-06` closure must not be read as that proof. It found a return re-entering stock at zero cost, now valued from the item’s persisted current valuation state; original FIFO layer provenance and reversal are not modelled or proven. HANDOFF: `BUY-07` (material delivery to work package) is now COMPLETE, and it does NOT discharge this capability's handoff layer — reconciled rather than inherited. `BUY-06`'s canonical issue is coded to a project and a BOQ item and names NO work package, and by the frozen `BUY-07` invariant such a movement is explicitly ineligible for the work-package receipt: it cannot be acknowledged, cannot generate an acknowledgement, and cannot satisfy a next-role receipt (`modules/inventory/src/domain/delivery-acknowledgement.test.ts` refuses it as `not_a_work_package_delivery`). So no named next role receives a `BUY-06` issue, the cross-role handoff proof this layer asks for does not exist for its own shape, and `handoff` stays UNVERIFIED. Promoting it on a neighbour's evidence would be inheriting a proof rather than holding one. No migration was needed: the ledger and movement tables already exist, RLS enabled and forced.");
// Honest per layer: permissions proves only the unauthenticated refusal, and the handoff (BUY-07) is open.
for (const layer of ['domain','persistence','api','ui','actualOutput','browser']) caps.find(c=>c.id==='BUY-06').layers[layer]='COMPLETE';
caps.find(c=>c.id==='BUY-06').layers.permissions='PARTIAL';

// BUY-07 — the Store → Site handoff, proved with two principals on the product surface. All eight
// layers claimed, including `handoff`: the receipt IS the handoff, and it is the clause that was
// hardest to earn rather than the one most easily assumed.
set('BUY-07','COMPLETE',"MATERIAL REACHES A WORK PACKAGE, AND THE PERSON RESPONSIBLE FOR IT SIGNS FOR IT. The handoff starts at the ISSUE rather than the receipt: material arriving at a warehouse has not reached a work package, and the issue is the movement that physically crosses Store → Site. Procurement already carried the work package faithfully — a requisition line (0335) and a purchase order line (0336) both hold a validated `wbs_node_id` — and Inventory had never heard of one, so the coding was captured when material was REQUESTED and lost the moment it MOVED. Migration 0339 gives the movement its destination, validated through a declared port so the work-package structure stays Projects' (ADR-0004); the port is a validator only and offers no reverse lookup, because resolving a destination from a BOQ item would manufacture provenance out of its absence. A PROJECT ISSUE DOES NOT INHERENTLY MEAN DELIVERY TO A WORK PACKAGE: `BUY-06`'s authority is extended rather than redefined, an issue naming no package stays valid and unattributed, and that absence is reported ONCE at project level as “issued to project — work package not specified” instead of being attached to every package as UNKNOWN. THE RECEIPT WRITES NO QUANTITY, structurally — the acknowledgement table has no quantity column, and coverage is counted in MOVEMENTS — because what was delivered is derived from the movements and must keep one authority. NO RECIPIENT IS AN UNKNOWN, NOT AN OPEN DOOR: where nobody holds site execution for a package the handoff refuses, never falling back to the project-wide assignee nor to whoever holds a site role. The recipient authority itself did not exist and was added where operational ownership already lives (0340), and 0341 gives a stock movement the `issued_by` it never had, without which segregation of duties could not be evaluated at all.","JUDGED AGAINST THE FROZEN GENERIC CRITERION, restated here so it is visibly satisfied rather than replaced: “Representative Procurement Manager / Buyer, Storekeeper role executes Material delivery to work package in the canonical Procurement / Inventory context; save/reload, applicable permission denials, actual output and next-role receipt are proved.” No acceptance criterion was invented after the fact; the discovery that preceded this is evidence, not criterion. CANONICAL EXECUTION, BROWSER, AUTH-ON, TWO NAMED PEOPLE EACH SIGNED IN AS THEMSELVES: `u-e2e-storekeeper` (`r-store`) issues 40 m to a project and work package from the screen; `u-e2e-site` (`r-site-engineer`) signs in, sees the delivery, and accepts it. Both hold roles AURA already ships, unmodified — the fixture was changed to look like real usage, never the authorization. SAVE/RELOAD: the receipt survives a reload because it is read from the server, not component state; an earlier version kept it in the browser and the screen FORGOT it, which is indistinguishable from never having acknowledged. PERMISSION DENIALS, statuses read off the HTTP layer rather than inferred from wording — no inventory permission 403 naming the missing permission; not the responsible person 409; nobody responsible 409; the issuer signing for himself 400; a second receipt 409; an issue naming no work package 400; unauthenticated 401 — and every path asserted under 500. ACTUAL OUTPUT: `inventory.delivery.acknowledged`, asserted as PERSISTED on the spine rather than emitted, carrying who accepted, what, when and WHO ISSUED IT so the segregation of duties stays auditable after the fact; nothing is emitted when a receipt is refused. NEXT-ROLE RECEIPT: proved end to end on the product surface and confirmed from the spine, where `issuedBy` is the storekeeper and `acknowledgedBy` is the site engineer and the two are asserted to differ — read back rather than inferred from both sessions passing. DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED ON THE WAY, all introduced by this work and none visible to review: the issue button required a BOQ item, so delivering to a work package with no BOQ linkage — explicitly valid — was UNREACHABLE FROM THE SCREEN, the same defect shape as `BUY-06`; the receipt was component state; `onDone` called a TOGGLE and collapsed the row the moment a receipt was recorded; and an invented `inventory.delivery.acknowledge` permission would have made the next-role receipt impossible for the actual next role, since a Site Engineer holds `inventory.*.read` — accepting material is discharging the responsibility you hold, so it is governed by the existing `projects.responsibility.update` that both roles genuinely have. TWO REFUSALS RETURNED 500 while the static error-taxonomy gate reported the capability clean: that gate scans throw-statement literals and a reason composed in a domain function is invisible to it — the blind spot recorded during `BUY-01`, found here by the Auth-ON e2e and treated as the API contract defect it was. PERSISTENCE: `aura_inventory_delivery_acknowledgements` RLS ENABLED and FORCED with a policy, on a database rebuilt from zero (270 tenant-scoped tables, all enabled, forced and policied). ACCEPTED LIMITS, stated rather than absorbed: the browser specs pass individually and serially (`--workers=1`) and INTERFERE IN PARALLEL through the shared stock page and database — recorded, not fixed. `material_consumption` and `site_installations` still identify material as free text and are downstream consumption/installation debt, deliberately outside this capability. And for a FIFO item a return re-enters at the current persisted valuation state rather than restoring the original layer; original FIFO layer provenance and reversal are not modelled or proven.");
for (const layer of ['domain','persistence','api','permissions','ui','actualOutput','browser','handoff']) caps.find(c=>c.id==='BUY-07').layers[layer]='COMPLETE';

// SUP-01 — the internal technical verdict on a supplier's offer. All eight layers: the decision,
// its output on the spine, and its receipt by the Buyer are each proved with two real principals
// against the real database.
set('SUP-01','COMPLETE',"A SUPPLIER'S OFFER IS NOW JUDGED BY SOMEBODY WITH THE AUTHORITY TO JUDGE IT. A quotation was a header with one scalar `amount` and a free-text supplier name, so technical compliance had nowhere to exist — the fourth appearance in this wave of a header with a scalar where the business needs items. Migration 0344 gives the quotation lines at the grain of the REQUISITION LINE, and 0345 gives each offer an internal verdict recorded BESIDE it. The separation is the capability: the line carries only what the SUPPLIER DECLARES — make and model offered, deviations, exclusions and their own compliance response — and there is deliberately no `is_compliant` column, because a supplier writing “comply” is a claim with exactly the standing of their price. The verdict is an INTERNAL determination, governed by `engineering.technical-evaluation.decide`, which in the shipped role catalogue only the Technical Manager holds; the Buyer records offers and cannot decide compliance. AN OFFER NOBODY HAS EVALUATED IS UNKNOWN — not eligible, not ineligible, and never a reason to prefer it for being cheap — and the eligibility read never falls back to the supplier's own claim. A verdict costs a RATIONALE and is amended by SUPERSESSION rather than overwritten, so the replaced decision keeps its own reasoning. A quantity differing from the one requested is a DEVIATION, derived from the requisition line and the offer and surfaced to the evaluator rather than decided for them. Quality's MAR is a SEPARATE authority that this path does not read; whether an approved MAR is even required for a given material is undetermined and deliberately not assumed.","JUDGED AGAINST THE FROZEN GENERIC CRITERION, restated so it is visibly satisfied rather than replaced: “Representative Procurement Manager / Buyer, Technical Manager, Commercial Manager / QS role executes Technical compliance in the canonical Procurement RFQ context; save/reload, applicable permission denials, actual output and next-role receipt are proved.” CANONICAL EXECUTION, BROWSER, AUTH-ON, AGAINST THE REAL DATABASE, TWO NAMED PEOPLE each signed in as themselves and each holding a role AURA already ships: `u-e2e-techmgr` (`r-technical-manager`, `engineering.*`, NO procurement permission) opens their own evaluation queue, sees the requirement beside the offer and a −2 quantity deviation, and records a verdict; `u-e2e-buyer` (`r-procurement`) works the quotation. SAVE/RELOAD: the verdict survives a reload because it is the server's, and an amendment keeps both decisions. PERMISSION DENIALS: the Buyer is refused the decision 403 NAMING the missing permission, refused the evaluator's view and refused the work queue, while still reading eligibility; unauthenticated 401. The permission itself was corrected during the work — an invented `inventory.delivery.acknowledge`-style choice would have made the determination impossible for the only role allowed to make it, so it is governed by an `engineering.*` permission the Technical Manager genuinely holds. ACTUAL OUTPUT: `procurement.quotation_line.evaluated` asserted as PERSISTED on the spine — verdict, rationale, who decided, when, and the superseded id so an amendment is legible AS an amendment. NEXT-ROLE RECEIPT: the Technical Manager decides on their surface, and the Buyer opens their own quotation and sees eligibility, verdict and WHO DECIDED IT, under the Buyer's own permission — while still being refused the reasoning. The decision reaches the next role where that role already works, which is the handoff; an endpoint the Buyer could have called is not. DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED, all introduced by this work: `company_id` was added to the quotation type and the Postgres store but never to a migration, so every real insert failed; and the verdict amendment inserted the replacement BEFORE superseding the previous one, which the partial unique index correctly refused. Both passed twenty API tests and failed instantly against the real database. A wording overclaim was also corrected in code and schema comments: MAR had been described as “evidence a technical evaluator may consult” when nothing reads it. ACCEPTED LIMITS, stated rather than absorbed: the API e2e suite runs on IN-MEMORY stores (`vitest.config.e2e.ts` sets `DATABASE_URL: ''`), so its 25 passing tests prove the HTTP, auth and domain contract and NOT persistence — persistence here is proved by the browser suite against the real database and by RLS. There is no CROSS-SUPPLIER COMPARISON screen: the Buyer sees the verdict per offer on one quotation. That is deliberate and not a gap in this capability — a comparison surface is a later capability that CONSUMES this result, and requiring it here would make technical compliance depend on supplier recommendation, which the frozen criterion does not ask for. `lowestQuote` is untouched and still compares un-normalised scalars; SUP-06 and SUP-13 remain open.");
for (const layer of ['domain','persistence','api','permissions','ui','actualOutput','browser','handoff']) caps.find(c=>c.id==='SUP-01').layers[layer]='COMPLETE';
