 roles:['Procurement Manager / Buyer','Technical Manager','Commercial Manager / QS'],
 stages:['Procurement','Pre-Sales'],
 authority:'Procurement RFQ',
 currentBehavior:'The quotation LINE API is complete and the line SCREEN is read-only — it contains no POST at all, so every priced line proved during SUP-01 and SUP-06 was created through the API. The quotation HEADER screen sends three fields (supplierName, amount, leadTimeDays) and therefore captures NONE of the commercial facts SUP-06 normalises on: currency, tax treatment, tax rate, freight, payment terms and validity are all absent, so a quotation entered on screen is correctly reported as “the tax treatment was never stated” for every line. Three facts have no home even in the API — quotation reference, quotation date and source attachment — and neither does a part number, nor a split between technical and commercial deviation. TWO LIVE DEFECTS sit inside this: the header route carries no explicit @Permissions, so the guard derives `procurement.rfq.quotes`, which NO shipped role holds — a Buyer with procurement.*.create does not match a third segment of `quotes`, so only an administrator can record a quotation at all; and the RFQ form still offers a “Lead days” input that the API now explicitly refuses, because migration 0346 moved lead time onto the line. Worst of all, a supplier revision is DESTRUCTIVE: RfqQuoteStatus is received|rejected|awarded with no draft, no revision, no version and no supersede, and the only write path is a whole-object overwrite, so Rev 0 at 100 followed by Rev 1 at 92 leaves one row saying 92 and no evidence that 100 was ever offered. Quotation lines already have a supersede model from SUP-01; the header has none.',
 expectedOperationalBehavior:'A Buyer captures a complete supplier quotation from the screen — header commercial facts, lines, attachment and reference — under a permission their role actually holds, and a supplier revision creates a new immutable revision rather than overwriting the offer it replaces.',
