 classification:'WRONG_BEHAVIOR',
 kind:'fresh defect \u2014 SEC-01 stage 3, wave C: the seven document-control governing acts, plus the engineering route that bypassed them',
 status:'OPEN',
 roles:['Document Controller','Technical Manager','Design / Technical Engineer'],
 stages:['Engineering','Handover'],
 authority:'modules/doccontrol/src/domain/document-revision.ts; modules/doccontrol/src/domain/transmittal.ts; modules/doccontrol/src/domain/correspondence.ts; modules/doccontrol/src/domain/submittal.ts; modules/doccontrol/src/doccontrol.service.ts; apps/api/src/doccontrol/doccontrol.controller.ts; apps/api/src/events/drawing-transmittal-subscriber.ts',
 currentBehavior:'NO SHIPPED ROLE HELD A SINGLE `doccontrol` WRITE PERMISSION. All 24 roles held `doccontrol.*.read` and nothing else, so 19 mutating routes and seven governing verbs were reachable only through an administrator\u2019s wildcard, and THERE WAS NO DOCUMENT CONTROLLER ROLE IN THE CATALOGUE AT ALL. The revision record itself was well built \u2014 five separate actor columns with five timestamps, a strict state machine (`submitted \u2192 under_review \u2192 approved \u2192 issued`, no shortcut), a mandatory rejection reason, and supersede-not-overwrite already true. What no rule connected was WHO may stand in each column. Measured against the running API as one principal: `201 submit / 201 start-review / 201 approve / 201 issue`, with `submittedBy = decidedBy = issuedBy = u-admin`. One person authored, reviewed, approved and released the same drawing to the outside world, and every status along the way was correct. WORSE, THE APPROVAL FABRICATED THE REVIEW: `approveDocument` back-filled `reviewedBy: actorId ?? d.reviewedBy`, so the approver became the reviewer retroactively and a two-stage review showed two names where one act had happened. THE OTHER THREE RECORDS RECORDED LESS THAN THE REVISION DID. A TRANSMITTAL is the act of a document leaving the building and `sendTransmittal(t)` took no actor \u2014 the row kept `sent_at` and no `sent_by`, and `sender` is a free-text addressee label that resolves to nobody; its RECIPIENTS were correctly frozen at `sent` but its ITEMS were not, so attaching a document to an already-sent transmittal returned 201 and the list of WHAT was conveyed could change after the conveyance. CORRESPONDENCE close recorded nobody, asked for no reason and had no state guard: closing an already-closed item returned 200 and silently overwrote the first account of it. A SUBMITTAL kept `submitted_at` and `returned_at` with no actor on either. AND THERE WAS A SECOND ROUTE TO A SENT TRANSMITTAL THAT OBEYED NONE OF IT: `POST engineering/drawings/:id/transmit` emits an event whose reactor CREATES A DOCCONTROL TRANSMITTAL AND SENDS IT, passing `actorId: null` to both calls \u2014 commented at the call site as \u201csystem-initiated conveyance: no createdBy \u2192 no cross-module permission coupling\u201d. Both service methods skip their permission check when no actor is supplied, so DECLINING TO NAME THE ACTOR WAS THE MECHANISM THAT SKIPPED THE GUARD: an engineer holding not one document-control permission produced a sent conveyance signed by nobody, in the same table and with the same status as one the Document Controller had released to the client, with nothing to tell them apart. The route also ACCEPTED `transmittalRef` FROM THE REQUEST BODY, letting a release claim a conveyance reference that nothing verified.',
 expectedOperationalBehavior:'Three hands on a controlled document: the engineer writes and submits it, the Technical Manager reviews and approves it and releases nothing, the Document Controller registers and issues it and approves nothing. An approval never stands in for a review. A sent transmittal is a record of what went out and cannot be edited \u2014 a correction is a new transmittal. Closing correspondence says who, when and why, once. Submittal acts name their actor. The engineering handoff stays with engineering, is marked as internal, is signed, and cannot produce a conveyance to the client.',
 evidence:'Live probe against a restarted API on the built bundle (26 assertions, three real principals); infrastructure/migrations/0371 and 0372; modules/doccontrol/src/domain/document-revision.ts (`revisionSeparation`); modules/doccontrol/src/domain/transmittal.ts (`TransmittalKind`, `isExternalConveyance`, `sentBy`); modules/doccontrol/src/domain/correspondence.ts; modules/doccontrol/src/domain/submittal.ts (`submittalProvenance`); modules/doccontrol/src/doccontrol.service.ts (`releaseInternally`); apps/api/src/doccontrol/doccontrol.controller.ts (19 declared permissions); core/src/identity/standard-elv-roles.ts (DOCCONTROL_AUTHOR / _TECHNICAL_AUTHORITY / _RELEASE_AUTHORITY, and r-document-controller, a new role); apps/api/src/doccontrol/release-authority.security.test.ts; modules/doccontrol/src/release-provenance.pg-int.test.ts.',
 severity:'CRITICAL',
