 currentBehavior:'THE FIRST WAVE WITH NO SINGLE WILDCARD AT ITS CENTRE: these twelve acts sat behind FOUR \u2014 `site.*` on the Site Engineer, `quality.*` on QA/QC, `commissioning.handover.*` on Handover/FM and `commissioning.record.*` on the T&C Engineer \u2014 and the defect behind each is different in kind. THE DAY. Measured against the running API as ONE Site Engineer: `201 POST site/daily-reports preparedBy=u-e2e-site`, `200 PUT :id/submit submittedBy=u-e2e-site`, `201 POST :id/start-review reviewedBy=u-e2e-site`, `201 POST :id/approve approvedBy=u-e2e-site`. One person prepared, submitted, reviewed and approved the contemporaneous record of what happened on site \u2014 the document a delay claim, a variation and a payment dispute are later argued from. The record was BUILT for the check: four actor columns, four timestamps, and nothing connecting them. `rejectReport` also wrote `reviewedBy: actorId ?? r.reviewedBy`, so a rejecter OVERWROTE whoever performed the review. AND THE SERVICE SPOKE A DIFFERENT VOCABULARY FROM THE ROUTES: it asserted `site.daily_report.*` with an UNDERSCORE while the guard derived `site.daily-report.*` with a HYPHEN, and `site.*` matched both, so nothing could see it. A SITE INSTRUCTION carries cost and time implications and its `issued_by` is FREE TEXT \u2014 measured, the string "Anyone I Like" was accepted \u2014 while acknowledging and closing it recorded nobody. A SNAG HAD NO STATE MACHINE AT ALL: `status` was a plain column the service assigned to in place, and measured, `PUT :id/close` returned 200 and then `PUT :id/resolve` returned 200 on the same snag \u2014 A CLOSED DEFECT WALKED BACKWARDS. Closing recorded no actor, no timestamp, and did not set `resolved_at` on the way past. AN ITP \u2014 the plan that says what must be inspected, witnessed and held \u2014 was activated and closed by functions taking no actor and asserting nothing in the service; the only gate was the derived route name. THE HANDOVER PACKAGE HAD NO ACTOR COLUMNS WHATSOEVER: `submitted_at` and `accepted_at` with nobody attached, `accept()` taking no actor parameter at all, and its audit event attributed to `next.created_by` \u2014 SO THE CLIENT\u2019S ACCEPTANCE, WHICH STARTS THE WARRANTY AND DEFECTS-LIABILITY CLOCK, WAS FILED AGAINST WHOEVER CREATED THE PACKAGE. Measured, the other half: `403 PUT commissioning/handovers/:id/submit` as the PROJECT MANAGER \u2014 "no grant satisfies commissioning.handover.submit" \u2014 because `commissioning.handover.*` sat only on Handover/FM, so the party RECEIVING the handover was the only party that could issue it. Finally, `openTransmittalFor` wrapped the doccontrol call in a catch that returned null for ANY error including AccessDenied, so a dossier could be issued to the client with a 200 response, no conveyance record, and a line in the server log.',
