 authority:'modules/projects/src/delay-eot.service.ts; modules/projects/src/domain/delay-eot.ts; modules/projects/src/project.service.ts; apps/api/src/projects/projects.controller.ts',
 currentBehavior:'`assertProjectAccess` in delay-eot.service.ts asserted `projects.project.update` \u2014 "edit the project" \u2014 for FIVE different acts: creating a delay event, assessing it, raising an EOT claim, submitting that claim, and DETERMINING it. Measured against the shipped role catalogue: r-commercial-manager NAMES `projects.eot-claim.*` and does NOT hold `projects.project.update`; r-planning-engineer NAMES `projects.delay.*` and does NOT hold it either; r-pm holds `projects.*` and therefore holds everything. So the role whose job an extension-of-time claim IS could not touch one \u2014 403 against the running API, creating a claim in its own module \u2014 the Planning Engineer could not assess the delay behind it, and the single role that could do either submitted a claim and determined the same claim. This is the shape wave B removed from the permit to work, where ASKING for a permit required the authority to GRANT one. AND THE CLAIM RECORDED NOBODY WHO MADE IT: `aura_projects_eot_claims` has `submitted_at`, `decided_at` and `decided_by`, and NO `created_by` AND NO `submitted_by`. An EOT claim is a formal contractual position that moves the completion date and carries money with it, and it was written by nobody and submitted by nobody. Where an actor WAS recorded, the controller wrote `decidedBy: ctx.actorId ?? \u2018system\u2019`, so an unauthenticated determination was attributed to a principal named "system" that exists in no roster \u2014 the same fabrication wave C removed when an approval was allowed to stand in for a review that never happened. Nothing stopped a determination being recorded against a claim still in draft, which would mean answering something the client was never sent. CANCELLING A PROJECT IS THE COUNTER-EXAMPLE and is well built: the service refuses without an actor AND without a reason, and evaluates a real lifecycle transition first. Both facts go into the event payload and NEITHER goes onto the project row, which afterwards reads `status = cancelled` and nothing else \u2014 so anyone reading the project rather than replaying the event store sees that it was abandoned and cannot see by whom or why.',
 expectedOperationalBehavior:'Each act asks for its own permission, so the role that names a capability can exercise it. Commercial/QS prepares and submits an extension-of-time claim and determines none; the PM records the determination that comes back, because it moves the completion date, and submits none. A determination answers a claim that was actually sent, and an unsigned one is recorded as unsigned rather than attributed to an invented principal. A cancelled project carries who cancelled it and why on the record, not only in the event stream.',
