 currentBehavior:'A Buyer captures a supplier quotation from the screen, prices its items, and the comparison reads exactly what they typed. A quotation is an immutable FAMILY (supplier + RFQ) holding one or more OFFERS — a base offer and any technical alternatives — each with an append-only chain of immutable REVISIONS. A supplier’s change creates the next revision and never overwrites the one before it, so Rev 0 at 500 freight, Rev 1 at 500 and Rev 2 at none all survive, with a diff on screen saying what moved. Exactly one revision per offer is commercially effective, enforced by a PARTIAL UNIQUE INDEX on status = ‘confirmed’ with the transition applied as a demote-then-promote inside one transaction. Expiry is DERIVED against the comparison date and never stored, because a revision expired today was live on a June comparison. An offer with no effective revision — withdrawn, rejected, or captured and never confirmed — appears in SUP-06 carrying the reason and is NEVER silently omitted, and an earlier revision is never reinstated: a supplier who pulls Rev 2 has not re-offered Rev 1. A technical alternative is a separate OFFER, so a Bosch equivalent cannot replace the Hikvision offer it was quoted beside. Lines belong to the revision that quoted them, carrying supplier description, make, model, part number, quantity, unit price, discount, per-item lead time and technical and commercial deviations kept apart. Both live defects this record named are fixed: the capture routes declare permissions a Buyer actually holds, where the old route derived `procurement.rfq.quotes` that NO shipped role held, and the refused “Lead days” input is gone. Every legacy field is retired — quotation_id and is_alternate dropped, revision_id NOT NULL — and SUP-01’s verdicts now key on the revision, which the quotation could not express: a verdict on Rev 1’s line could previously be read as a verdict on Rev 2’s different price.',
