Object.assign(defects.find(d=>d.id==='HR-APR-01'),{
 classification:'COMPLETE',
 status:'CLOSED_VERIFIED',
 acceptanceProof:"MET. THE DEPARTMENT HAS ROLES: `r-hr` (HR Officer) captures the employee master, leave, attendance, timesheets and appraisals and PREPARES payroll; `r-hr-manager` approves timesheets, expense claims, staff advances and appraisals, and pays nothing; Finance takes the three money-out acts — `hr.payroll.pay`, `hr.expense-claim.reimburse`, `hr.staff-advance.disburse` — and approves none of the entitlements. All 28 routes declare a permission. PROVENANCE: every transition records its actor, including the two that recorded nobody at all — the rejection and the payment. DOMAIN: the employee may not approve their own claim, advance or hours, and the approver may not release the payment; the comparison resolves the employee’s LINKED ACCOUNT, because `employeeId` is an employee record and the actor is a user id, and where there is no link the act proceeds and says `unverifiable` rather than blocking an employee’s expenses over a link nobody set up. THE UUID DEFECT: `approved_by` was UUID on all three tables, so approval returned `400 invalid input syntax for type uuid` for every user including the administrator — measured, fixed in 0369, and re-measured green. A SIGNATURE HAZARD WAS REMOVED ALONG THE WAY: `reimburseClaim` took a DATE second, and adding an actor there meant an existing caller silently passed the date as the payer — both are `string`, so TypeScript could not see it. One caller did exactly that. The trailing arguments are an options object now, and the compiler immediately caught the same mistake in a second test, which is the difference between fixed and unrepresentable. LIVE, against a restarted API on the built bundle: create employee 201, raise claim 201, submit 201 (`submittedBy` recorded), Finance refused the approval 403, approve 201, THE APPROVER REFUSED THE REIMBURSEMENT 403, Finance reimburses 201 (`reimbursedBy` recorded). REGRESSION: verified with NO ADDITIONAL KNOWN SUITE REGRESSIONS OVER BASELINE. apps/api unit 635 passed / 4 skipped; hr 74 passed; apps/api e2e 14 files and 39 tests failing with 567 passing, identical to the HEAD baseline. NOT CLAIMED: appraisals and end-of-service got declared permissions and roles in the same pass; their own maker/checker questions have not been asked.",
});

Object.assign(defects.find(g=>g.id==='SEC-01'),{
 currentBehavior:(defects.find(g=>g.id==='SEC-01')?.currentBehavior??'')+' WAVE A IS COMPLETE. 320 mutating ungoverned routes and 40 governing verbs, from 423 and 66 when the guard was written. Every governing verb in subcontracts, finance, procurement, tendering, HR and contracts is gone; the 40 that remain are in modules this programme has not reached — hse 8, doccontrol 7, commissioning 4, projects 4, site 4, crm 3, quality 3, and one or two each in amc, assets, engineering, fleet, intelligence and inventory. A FIFTH LESSON, and the one that changes how the remaining waves should be read: A WILDCARD DOES NOT ONLY OVER-GRANT, IT HIDES WHETHER THE THING IT GRANTS WORKS. Three times now an act reachable only through an administrator turned out to be BROKEN for every user — `aura_subcontracts_variations.approved_by` and then all three HR `approved_by` columns were UUID while every actor id in the system is text, so approving a variation, an expense claim, a staff advance or a timesheet had never once succeeded. The raw PostgreSQL type error reached the client as a 400 that read like bad input. Nobody walked those paths because nobody could.',
 acceptanceProof:(defects.find(g=>g.id==='SEC-01')?.acceptanceProof??'')+' STAGE 3 WAVE A: CLOSED. Six modules, 23 governing acts, five new records (SUB-CERT-01, AR-INV-01, PROC-COM-01, HR-APR-01 and the tendering/contracts work folded into this one), three new roles (r-finance-controller, r-hr, r-hr-manager) and seven migrations (0364–0369). NEXT: wave B is `hse` — eight acts behind ONE wildcard, `hse.*` on r-hse, which is the best ratio left in the plan and the only wave where safety rather than money is the consequence.',
});

